        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 14,
        "titulo": "«Mi piace»: the thing you like is the subject",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: English says «I like coffee» with «I» as the subject. Italian says «Mi piace il caffè», literally «Coffee pleases me». The subject is the thing you like, and you are the person it pleases. STEP 2: the verb agrees with the thing, not with you. One thing: «piace». More than one: «piacciono». «Mi piace la pizza», «Mi piacciono i film italiani». STEP 3: when what you like is a verb in the infinitive, use «piace» (singular): «Mi piace leggere», «Mi piace viaggiare e cucinare». STEP 4: the person is shown by a small pronoun: «mi» (to me), «ti» (to you), «gli» (to him), «le» (to her), «Le» (to you, formal), «ci» (to us), «vi» (to you plural), «gli» (to them; the formal alternative is «loro», placed after the verb). STEP 5: the negative puts «non» before the pronoun: «Non mi piacciono le olive». STEP 6: with a common noun, the article is needed: «Mi piace il caffè», not «Mi piace caffè». English drops the article here («I like coffee»). STEP 7: for something you love, use «adoro» (I adore) or «amo»; for something you cannot stand, use «detesto». Be careful: «amo» is strong and is often used for people, so with things «adoro» is more common. STEP 8: to say how much, use «molto» (a lot), «abbastanza» (fairly), «non molto» (not much), «per niente» (not at all). STEP 9: pronunciation: «piace» is PYAH-cheh and «piacciono» is PYAHT-choh-noh.",
        "comparacao": "This is the best-known change of subject for English speakers. English has «I like» (subject = me). Italian has «piace» (subject = the thing). So a plural thing makes the verb plural, as in «Mi piacciono le olive». The short translation to memorize is «X pleases me». Do not translate «I like» word for word as «Io piaccio», which means «I am liked».",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Mi piace leggere",
            "pt": "I like reading"
          },
          {
            "ru": "Mi piacciono i film italiani",
            "pt": "I like Italian movies"
          },
          {
            "ru": "Non mi piacciono le olive",
            "pt": "I don't like olives"
          }
